{#
    The cascade: on load, the page's content arrives as one unbroken run, each
    block a beat after the one above it, top to bottom across every section.
    The continuity is the point; staggering whole sections instead reads as a
    flicker.

    The script walks the main column in document order, descending through
    containers (the article, its sections and headers, the prose, the lists)
    and treating everything else as one unit: a paragraph, a figure, a list
    row, a section bar. Each unit above the fold gets its place in the run as
    --cascade-i and the class that animates it (global.css, "Cascade").
    Units below the fold are left alone, so nothing animates out of sight
    and the run's length is bounded by the screen, not by the page.

    It runs at the foot of the body, before the first paint, so a unit is
    never seen before it starts. Without the script nothing moves and the
    page is simply there. It stands down for reduced motion and for a page
    brought back with the back button, which the reader has already seen.
#}
<script>
(function () {
    if (matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) return;
    var nav = performance.getEntriesByType("navigation")[0];
    if (nav && nav.type === "back_forward") return;

    var containers = "article, section, header, .page-content, .post-content, .margin-list, .facts, .list-header, .books-grid, .directory-list";
    var fold = innerHeight;
    var i = 0;

    (function walk(el) {
        for (var c = el.firstElementChild; c; c = c.nextElementSibling) {
            if (c.matches(containers)) { walk(c); continue; }
            var r = c.getBoundingClientRect();
            if (r.top >= fold) return;
            if (!r.height) continue;
            c.style.setProperty("--cascade-i", i++);
            c.classList.add("cascade");
        }
    })(document.querySelector("main"));
})();
</script>
